# ============================================================================
# P05 - BUILD: enable ThinLTO                   [EXPERIMENTAL / UNVERIFIED]
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IT CHANGES
#   CONFIG_LTO_THIN=y  (thin-link-time optimization, LLVM)
#
#   baseline evidence (kernel source, commit 9b1c9a53):
#     k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig:22   CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE=y
#     grep -in lto k65v1_64_bsp_defconfig  ->  NO MATCH
#     grep -in lto oppo6765_defconfig      ->  NO MATCH
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:60       select ARCH_SUPPORTS_LTO_CLANG
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:61       select ARCH_SUPPORTS_THINLTO
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:118      select HAVE_ARCH_PREL32_RELOCATIONS if !LTO_CLANG
#   So: the toolchain support is selected, but LTO is OFF in both MT6765
#   defconfigs. The baseline already builds with -Os (CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE).
#
# WHY IT SHOULD HELP ON A 4 GB MT6765V
#   ThinLTO is the LTO mode designed for exactly this constraint. Full LTO
#   needs the whole link in one LLVM module, which for a vendor kernel means
#   either a giant link or a lot of RAM. ThinLTO splits into ~100-unit
#   partitions linked with a full LTO index, so peak linker memory stays
#   bounded - which matters on a build host, and is why AOSP GKI ships
#   ThinLTO rather than full LTO.
#   The runtime argument is code size and instruction-fetch behaviour. ThinLTO
#   sees across translation-unit boundaries, so it can inline and drop
#   functions that -Os alone cannot see across. The expectation to test is:
#     - smaller Image/vmlinux  -> less text+rodata resident in a 4 GB device
#     - better i-cache/iTLB hit rate -> fewer L1I/L1D refills on the 6 A55s,
#       which have small private caches and are the cores background work and
#       most UI compositing actually run on
#     - slightly less work at boot (fewer pages to fault in)
#   On a big.LITTLE A76+A55 part the argument is specifically about the SMALL
#   cores' caches. That is a mechanism, not a measurement.
#
#   IMPORTANT: with CONFIG_CC_OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE already set, this is an
#   Os+LTO combination, which is the size-favouring corner. It is not a
#   claim of a faster kernel - the honest headline for this patch is
#   "smaller", with performance a secondary hypothesis to be measured.
#
# WHAT WOULD VERIFY OR REFUTE IT
#   1. Size - the cheapest, most certain test, do this first:
#      ls -l arch/arm64/boot/Image          (baseline vs patched, byte count)
#      nm -S --size-sort vmlinux | tail -20
#      If Image does not shrink, LTO did not engage and nothing else matters.
#   2. Boot time - see VALIDATION.md section 5 (logcat boot_progress), 10 cold
#      boots each, compare p50 and p95. Expect a small delta; a regression
#      would point at load-time work or relocation overhead.
#   3. Runtime:
#      dumpsys gfxinfo <pkg> framestats
#      cat /proc/pressure/cpu
#      If you have the hardware, perf stat -e L1-icache-load-misses,
#      L1-icache-loads, iTLB-load-misses over a fixed workload - this is the
#      metric the mechanism actually predicts. That is the real test.
#   4. Reject the patch if size did not drop, or if build risk proves
#      unmanageable on the vendor modules.
#
# RISK - HIGHEST IN THIS SET, READ BEFORE BUILDING
#   - MUST be built with Clang. GCC LTO is not enabled by this fragment.
#   - MTK 4.19 ships large out-of-tree trees, notably the Arm Bifrost GPU
#     driver (drivers/gpu/mediatek/gpu_mali/mali_bifrost/mali-r25p0/) and
#     the vendor/oplus/ tree. LTO requires every module to be LTO-clean
#     (correct -fno-lto handling of inline asm, __attribute__((section)),
#     and modversions/CONFIG_MODVERSIONS interaction). A module that is not
#     will fail to link.
#   - THIS PATCH HAS NEVER BEEN BUILT. Expect the first build attempt to
#     fail on some vendor module. Budget real time for that.
#   - If it will not link, the correct outcome is to DROP P05 and ship the
#     rest. Do not "fix" it by disabling CONFIG_LTO for selected objects
#     piecemeal - a half-LTO kernel defeats the point and hides breakage.
#   - Confirm CONFIG_MODVERSIONS interaction and that the vendor tree's
#     Kbuild/Makefiles do not force -ffreestanding/asm assumptions LTO breaks.
# ============================================================================

CONFIG_LTO_THIN=y
CONFIG_LTO=y
